THE IDENTITY
  (gross rent + other income) − agent fees − third-party outgoings = disbursed

A statement that ties is evidence: every figure has been read and the result
matches what reached the bank. A statement that does not tie usually means a
line was MISSED rather than that any single figure is wrong, which is why this
reports the gap instead of correcting anything.

DESIGN DECISIONS
- ADVISORY ONLY, never blocks a save. A balance brought forward from the
  previous statement legitimately breaks the identity; treating that as user
  error would train people to ignore the warning, which is worse than having no
  warning. There is an explicit test that it reports rather than throws.
- `applicable: false` is distinct from `ties: false`. No disbursed figure, or no
  statement detail at all (a bare email-ingested row), is UNKNOWN — not broken.
  Reporting unknown as a failure is the fastest way to make a check worthless.
- $1.00 tolerance. Fees are GST-inclusive and rounded per line so a cent or two
  of drift is normal; a dollar absorbs that while still failing on the smallest
  real fee line observed, a $4.40 bank charge. Both directions are tested.
- Other income is money IN alongside rent, NOT a reduction in fees. There is a
  test asserting totalMoneyIn is 4406.80 while totalFees stays 121.00, because
  netting it against fees would produce the right total for the wrong reason.
- `difference` is signed (computedNet − net_received) so a caller can say which
  way the statement is out, not merely that it is.

